name: c11 extras
intro: C11 added a handful of features that are small but earn their keep.
intro: Each solves a real problem you have already met.
intro:
intro: `_Static_assert(cond, "msg")` checks at compile time. Use it when two
intro: things must stay in step, so a mistake is a build error, not a bug:
intro:
intro:     _Static_assert(sizeof names / sizeof *names == N, "out of step");
intro:
intro: `_Generic(x, int: a, double: b, default: c)` picks an expression by
intro: the type of `x` -- at compile time, nothing is tested at run time. It
intro: is how a macro can print any type. Note `'x'` is an int in C, and a
intro: `const char *` is not a `char *`.
intro:
intro: A flexible array member is a last, unsized array in a struct. One
intro: malloc holds the header and the data together:
intro:
intro:     struct msg { size_t len; char data[]; };
intro:     struct msg *m = malloc(sizeof *m + n + 1);
intro:
intro: Free it once with one free. Only the last member can be like this.
intro:
intro: An anonymous struct or union has no name of its own, so its members
intro: read as the outer struct's: `v.i` instead of `v.u.i`. A tagged value
intro: is an enum saying which member of an anonymous union is live.
intro:
intro: `_Noreturn` marks a function that never comes back (it exits). The
intro: compiler then knows nothing runs after the call.
intro: `static inline` hints that a small function should be pasted into its
intro: caller; the compiler decides.
intro:
intro: `restrict` on a pointer promises that nothing else reaches that
intro: memory in this function -- you may then not pass overlapping
intro: arrays, and the compiler may reorder loads and stores freely. Break
intro: the promise and the behaviour is undefined. `memcpy` has it,
intro: `memmove` does not.
intro:
intro: C23 exists now and adds `nullptr`, `constexpr`, `#embed` and `bool`
intro: as a keyword. This compiler is C11, so this track stops at C11.
---
q: kind.c -- name the type
spec: The file starts with five variables and five printf calls; you write
spec: the macro `KIND(x)` with `_Generic` so each call prints the type name.
spec: Print exactly these six lines, in this order, and exit 0:
spec:
spec: `i: int`, `d: double`, `s: string`, `c: char`, `lit: int`, `u: other`
spec:
spec: `int` gives "int", `double` gives "double", `char` gives "char",
spec: `const char *` gives "string", anything else "other". The line `lit`
spec: is the character literal `'x'` itself, which has type int in C.
file: kind.c
start: #include <stdio.h>
start: 
start: #define KIND(x) ((void)(x), "?")
start: 
start: int main(void)
start: {
start: 	int i = 7;
start: 	double d = 2.5;
start: 	const char *s = "hi";
start: 	char c = 'x';
start: 	unsigned long u = 9;
start: 
start: 	printf("i: %s\n", KIND(i));
start: 	printf("d: %s\n", KIND(d));
start: 	printf("s: %s\n", KIND(s));
start: 	printf("c: %s\n", KIND(c));
start: 	printf("lit: %s\n", KIND('x'));
start: 	printf("u: %s\n", KIND(u));
start: 	return 0;
start: }
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: #define KIND(x) _Generic((x), \
prog: 	int: "int",               \
prog: 	double: "double",         \
prog: 	char: "char",             \
prog: 	const char *: "string",   \
prog: 	default: "other")
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	int i = 7;
prog: 	double d = 2.5;
prog: 	const char *s = "hi";
prog: 	char c = 'x';
prog: 	unsigned long u = 9;
prog: 
prog: 	printf("i: %s\n", KIND(i));
prog: 	printf("d: %s\n", KIND(d));
prog: 	printf("s: %s\n", KIND(s));
prog: 	printf("c: %s\n", KIND(c));
prog: 	printf("lit: %s\n", KIND('x'));
prog: 	printf("u: %s\n", KIND(u));
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: kind
case: kind; echo $?
case: kind | grep -c 'string'
hint: _Generic((x), int: "int", ...) -- list const char * for the string, and remember 'x' is an int
